-- title:
--   Antipode-stability passes to the join of two subalgebras
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $K$ a field which is an $R$-algebra, and $A$ a commutative ring carrying a Hopf algebra structure over $K$ and an $R$-algebra structure compatible with that of $K$ in the sense that $R \to K \to A$ is a scalar tower. Write $\mathcal S =$ `HopfAlgebra.antipode K` for the antipode of $A$ as a $K$-Hopf algebra, a $K$-linear endomorphism of $A$. Let $S$ and $S'$ be $R$-subalgebras of $A$, each stable under $\mathcal S$, i.e. $\mathcal S(x) \in S$ for all $x \in S$ and $\mathcal S(x) \in S'$ for all $x \in S'$. The conclusion is that the join $S \sqcup S'$ in the lattice of $R$-subalgebras of $A$ (the $R$-subalgebra generated by $S \cup S'$) is again stable under the antipode: $\mathcal S(x) \in S \sqcup S'$ for every $x \in S \sqcup S'$. Only the antipode-stability clause is involved; no finiteness, spanning, comultiplication or counit condition on $S$ or $S'$ is assumed or asserted.
--
--   This is the antipode clause of the statement that the join of two Hopf orders in a $K$-Hopf algebra $A$ over a subring $R$ of $K$ satisfies the defining conditions of a Hopf order, each clause being handled separately. It is used in the construction of a greatest Hopf order ([`HopfOrder.exists_isGreatest`](thm.html#HopfOrder.exists_isGreatest)) and in [`HopfAlgebra.existsUnique_bialgHom_baseChange_eq_of_pow_eq_one`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.existsUnique_bialgHom_baseChange_eq_of_pow_eq_one).
